  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nAs stated last night, forecast uncertainty remains quite high for\nstorm potential for the first part of next week. Guidance is in\ngood agreement with the synoptic set up for Sunday through early\nnext week, but it's the subtle mesoscale features that differ and\nwhere the main uncertainty lies. Models are also in better\nagreement with increasing moisture Sunday into Monday compared to\nlast night and this in turn is leading to higher forecast\ninstability and even PoPs. The ridge will continue to shift\nfarther to the east during this time as a trough moves into the\nNorthwestern U.S. The main shortwave within the trough will track\nacross the Northern Rockies, but the southern fringes of the \ntrough is still likely to bring increased support for showers and\nthunderstorms by early next week. Models show a seasonably strong\nupper level jet setting up from the Northern Rockies through the \nGreat Basin by Monday which is expected to place our region within\na favorable area for ascent as early as Sunday night and \npotentially lasting through next Tuesday or Wednesday. \n\nOther unknowns for early next week will be the placement of the \nbest moisture and likely the small swatch of the best instability.\nIt seems likely there will be considerable cloudiness somewhere \nover or near eastern Arizona which will limit instability, while\nthe best instability/lower CIN sets up in a more cloud-free area \nto the west (South-central AZ). There is also a decent chance of \nhaving some sort of mesoscale disturbance tracking northward out \nof Mexico at some point early next week which will help to \ninitiate storm activity. However, uncertainty is still too high to\nbe able to pinpoint the timing and the potential for storms, but \nthere is more optimism for at least one or two busy storm days \nearly next week. NBM PoPs have increased for early next week and \nlook fairly reasonable at anywhere from 30-70% across south- \ncentral and eastern Arizona. If moisture and instability ends up \non the higher end of guidance, we are likely to have to worry \nabout some strong storms and heavy rainfall/flooding potential. \nHow long this increased rain potential may last is also uncertain \nas models really don't change the synoptic set up through at least\nnext Thursday or Friday. It's doubtful we will have good rain \nchances each day next week, but it also seems likely there will \nnot be any strong drying that will affect the area.\n\nTemperatures next week are almost as uncertain as rain chances as\nfairly high model spread is seen from Sunday-Wednesday, but with\nat least a downtrend in temperatures. The latter half of next week\nshows even higher spread, but with an uptrend in temperatures with\nreadings eventually warming back to above normal by next weekend.",
